ntries, services, scheduled tasks, browser associations, and actual 360 component directories. `360se.exe` at `%APPDATA
%\360se6\Application` was 360.cn-signed; `C:\Program Files\Google\Chrome\Application\chrome.exe` was Google LLC-signed 
and was retained. [Task 1]
- Personal data stayed untouched: `%APPDATA%\360se6\User Data` and `%APPDATA%\360browser\bkinfo`. The cleanup covered p
rogram directories, startup/shortcut/uninstall/vendor-registry/file-association remnants, then stopped `360elam64`, del
eted its driver after identity/status checks, and removed orphaned `360bpsvc`. [Task 1]
- Completion evidence must independently cover absent specified paths, processes, normal services, tasks, shortcuts, st
artup entries, registry and associations; also confirm Chrome signature and Defender remain healthy. The recorded resul
t removed about 1.08 GiB and did not reboot. [Task 1]

## Failures and how to do differently

- An unusable uninstall UI is not a reason to continue Computer Use against a dead window -> pivot to the signed uninst
aller and a constrained PowerShell procedure. [Task 1]
- `Access denied` on protected `Program Files (x86)`/`ProgramData` remnants -> stop ordinary deletion, use the authoriz
ed administrator flow, then re-check all independent signals. [Task 1]
- An uninstaller exit code alone is insufficient -> verify paths, processes, services, startup, shortcuts, registry and
 file associations; before removing a driver, confirm it is stopped and validate path, product/company and SHA-256. [Ta
sk 1]

